/-!
Field measurements of clay as an instance of the ambiguous-observation rule.

Source: spreadsheet `CLAY_10_7490E.xlsx` (SHA-256 161bd7dc873d62d3d48a69ee55e2549c9a91f5c3c570c3055a093dfffd4b3c73),
sheet `Sheet1`, columns `sv' (kPa)` (the vertical stress the clay carries now) and
`sp' (kPa)` (the preconsolidation stress: the largest stress it has carried, as measured
from the clay today). 4319 rows record both; in 3330 of them the past stress is larger
than the present one. Those two counts were computed outside Lean.

The table below lists every present-stress value that is shared by rows with different
past stresses: 252 values. For each it gives the spreadsheet rows with the smallest
and the largest past stress. All stresses are in units of 1/1000000000000000 kPa, so that every
value is a whole number; the conversion from the spreadsheet's decimals is exact.

What is shown: for every one of the 252 listed values, two measured rows have the same
present stress and different past stress. So no rule that reads only the present stress
can give the past stress for every measured row.

What is NOT shown: that any two rows are the same clay with two histories (they are
usually different sites), that equal present stresses are more than a coincidence of
rounding in the source reports, or anything about the Hug model. The spreadsheet's
contents and the export are outside the kernel. No Mathlib is needed.
Not compiled when written.
-/
set_option autoImplicit false
set_option maxRecDepth 8192

namespace ClayMemoryInstance

/-- One shared present stress, with the two extreme past stresses recorded for it. -/
